// ============================================================================
// Hunt: NIKKE PC launcher supply-chain compromise - K2F remote-access agent
//   and crypto-wallet stealer delivered via trojaned launcher scripts
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1195.002 (Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software
//   Supply Chain), T1071.001 (Web Protocols), T1105 (Ingress Tool
//   Transfer), T1059 (Command and Scripting Interpreter - LuaJIT payload
//   and remote command execution), T1113 (Screen Capture), T1555.003
//   (Credentials from Web Browsers)
// Data: Microsoft Defender DeviceNetworkEvents, DeviceFileEvents,
//   DeviceImageLoadEvents, DeviceProcessEvents (Defender for Endpoint)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-8-2026.html
// Logic: On September 27, 2026, four JavaScript files on Level Infinite's
//   official CDN serving the Goddess of Victory: NIKKE PC launcher were
//   modified to inject a malicious loader. Whenever a player opened the
//   launcher, the loader downloaded additional code from
//   attacker-controlled domains - insightjs.com, falling back to
//   insightjs.org. The downloaded code abused the launcher's native
//   functionality to fetch a LuaJIT payload called insight.bundle, which
//   installed a remote-access agent referred to as K2F. K2F lets attackers
//   remotely execute commands, browse and download local files, capture
//   screenshots, collect NIKKE account credentials, and steal information
//   stored in web browsers. The malware could also install a Windows DLL,
//   platform_experience_helper.dll, that remained active after the launcher
//   was closed; strings in a sample uploaded to VirusTotal indicate it can
//   steal cryptocurrency wallet data from browser extensions including
//   MetaMask, Phantom, and Coinbase Wallet. Level Infinite shipped a fixed
//   client on November 4, 2026, so the exposure window is roughly five
//   weeks (September 27 - November 4). It is not known how many players
//   were affected, how the attackers gained write access to the CDN, or
//   whether the wallet-stealing DLL was ever deployed to infected machines,
//   so treat any hit as a confirmed compromise of that machine: update the
//   client, run a full malware scan, and change passwords and sensitive
//   credentials from a clean device. Variant 1 hunts connections to the
//   attacker domains. Variant 2 hunts the payload and DLL artifacts on
//   disk and in memory. Variant 3 hunts K2F's post-exploitation behaviour
//   (shell execution and browser credential access) on devices that talked
//   to the domains. The queries use no invented IoCs - no hashes or IPs
//   were published; the domains, filenames and behaviours are the ones
//   reported by BleepingComputer and independent researcher MotherSphere.
//   Remediation: update the NIKKE client to the November 4 fixed version,
//   treat any hit machine as compromised, and rotate credentials from a
//   clean device.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: network connections to the attacker domains --
// The trojaned launcher scripts reached out to insightjs.com, falling back
// to insightjs.org, every time the launcher was opened. Any connection to
// either domain from a device that runs the NIKKE launcher is the
// compromise - the domains have no legitimate purpose. The exposure window
// ran September 27 to November 4, 2026, but keep the window open-ended in
// case a stale client still phones home.
let MaliciousDomains = dynamic(["insightjs.com", "insightjs.org"]);
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where RemoteUrl has_any (MaliciousDomains)
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessFolderPath, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, RemotePort
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: K2F payload and wallet-stealer DLL artifacts --
// The loader fetched a LuaJIT payload named insight.bundle, and the
// malware could install platform_experience_helper.dll, which stayed
// active after the launcher closed. Hunt both artifacts as files on disk
// and as loaded images - either one on a machine is a confirmed hit.
let MaliciousFiles = dynamic(["insight.bundle", "platform_experience_helper.dll"]);
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where FileName in (MaliciousFiles)
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessFileName, SHA256
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- DLL loads (same artifacts, seen in memory) --
DeviceImageLoadEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where FileName in (MaliciousFiles)
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessFolderPath, SHA256
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: K2F post-exploitation behaviour on exposed devices --
// K2F executes remote commands and steals browser-stored information, and
// the DLL targets crypto-wallet browser extensions (MetaMask, Phantom,
// Coinbase Wallet). Take every device that connected to the attacker
// domains and look for shell interpreters spawning or processes touching
// browser credential stores on those devices in the same window.
let MaliciousDomains = dynamic(["insightjs.com", "insightjs.org"]);
let ExposedDevices =
    DeviceNetworkEvents
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
    | where RemoteUrl has_any (MaliciousDomains)
    | distinct DeviceName;
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where DeviceName in (ExposedDevices)
| where FileName in~ ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "wscript.exe", "cscript.exe", "mshta.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- browser credential-store access on the same exposed devices --
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where DeviceName in (ExposedDevices)
| where FileName in~ ("Login Data", "Web Data", "Login Data For Account")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
